WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.708
Aesop’s Fables. Part 4.

00:00:02.708 --> 00:00:04.401
The Moon and her Mother.

00:00:04.401 --> 00:00:07.435
The Moon once begged her Mother to make her a gown.

00:00:07.435 --> 00:00:11.687
"How can I?" replied she; "there's no fitting your figure.

00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:19.121
At one time you're a New Moon, and at another you're a Full Moon; and between whiles you're neither one nor the other."

00:00:19.121 --> 00:00:20.866
Mercury and the Woodman.

00:00:20.866 --> 00:00:30.169
A Woodman was felling a tree on the bank of a river, when his axe, glancing off the trunk, flew out of his hands and fell into the water.

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:47.842
As he stood by the water's edge lamenting his loss, Mercury appeared and asked him the reason for his grief; and on learning what had happened, out of pity for his distress he dived into the river and, bringing up a golden axe, asked him if that was the one he had lost.

00:00:47.842 --> 00:00:57.134
The Woodman replied that it was not, and Mercury then dived a second time, and, bringing up a silver axe, asked if that was his.

00:00:57.134 --> 00:01:00.769
"No, that is not mine either," said the Woodman.

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:05.974
Once more Mercury dived into the river, and brought up the missing axe.

00:01:05.974 --> 00:01:17.748
The Woodman was overjoyed at recovering his property, and thanked his benefactor warmly; and the latter was so pleased with his honesty that he made him a present of the other two axes.

00:01:17.748 --> 00:01:26.753
When the Woodman told the story to his companions, one of these was filled with envy of his good fortune and determined to try his luck for himself.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:34.546
So he went and began to fell a tree at the edge of the river, and presently contrived to let his axe drop into the water.

00:01:34.546 --> 00:01:44.873
Mercury appeared as before, and, on learning that his axe had fallen in, he dived and brought up a golden axe, as he had done on the previous occasion.

00:01:44.873 --> 00:02:05.734
Without waiting to be asked whether it was his or not the fellow cried, "That's mine, that's mine," and stretched out his hand eagerly for the prize: but Mercury was so disgusted at his dishonesty that he not only declined to give him the golden axe, but also refused to recover for him the one he had let fall into the stream.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:08.081
Honesty is the best policy.

00:02:08.081 --> 00:02:11.170
The Ass, the Fox, and the Lion.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:16.867
An Ass and a Fox went into partnership and sallied out to forage for food together.

00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:23.721
They hadn't gone far before they saw a Lion coming their way, at which they were both dreadfully frightened.

00:02:23.721 --> 00:02:37.965
But the Fox thought he saw a way of saving his own skin, and went boldly up to the Lion and whispered in his ear, "I'll manage that you shall get hold of the Ass without the trouble of stalking him, if you'll promise to let me go free."

00:02:37.965 --> 00:02:51.215
The Lion agreed to this, and the Fox then rejoined his companion and contrived before long to lead him by a hidden pit, which some hunter had dug as a trap for wild animals, and into which he fell.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:03:04.739
When the Lion saw that the Ass was safely caught and couldn't get away, it was to the Fox that he first turned his attention, and he soon finished him off, and then at his leisure proceeded to feast upon the Ass.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:09.155
Betray a friend, and you'll often find you have ruined yourself.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:11.036
The Lion and the Mouse.

00:03:11.651 --> 00:03:16.987
A Lion asleep in his lair was waked up by a Mouse running over his face.

00:03:16.987 --> 00:03:21.816
Losing his temper he seized it with his paw and was about to kill it.

00:03:21.816 --> 00:03:27.197
The Mouse, terrified, piteously entreated him to spare its life.

00:03:27.197 --> 00:03:32.725
"Please let me go," it cried, "and one day I will repay you for your kindness."

00:03:32.725 --> 00:03:43.307
The idea of so insignificant a creature ever being able to do anything for him amused the Lion so much that he laughed aloud, and good-humouredly let it go.

00:03:43.307 --> 00:03:46.394
But the Mouse's chance came, after all.

00:03:46.394 --> 00:03:56.457
One day the Lion got entangled in a net which had been spread for game by some hunters, and the Mouse heard and recognised his roars of anger and ran to the spot.

00:03:56.457 --> 00:04:04.053
Without more ado it set to work to gnaw the ropes with its teeth, and succeeded before long in setting the Lion free.

00:04:04.053 --> 00:04:13.074
"There!" said the Mouse, "you laughed at me when I promised I would repay you: but now you see, even a Mouse can help a Lion."

00:04:13.074 --> 00:04:14.857
The Crow and the Pitcher.

00:04:14.857 --> 00:04:28.025
A thirsty Crow found a Pitcher with some water in it, but so little was there that, try as she might, she could not reach it with her beak, and it seemed as though she would die of thirst within sight of the remedy.

00:04:28.025 --> 00:04:30.952
At last she hit upon a clever plan.

00:04:30.952 --> 00:04:42.297
She began dropping pebbles into the Pitcher, and with each pebble the water rose a little higher until at last it reached the brim, and the knowing bird was enabled to quench her thirst.

00:04:42.297 --> 00:04:44.911
Necessity is the mother of invention.
